----------------------------------------------------------------------
                     YAML Repeated Nodes Merge 
----------------------------------------------------------------------
                    No more copy three time and paste.
            Create an Anchor and reference that Anchor three time.
----------------------------------------------------------------------
# YAML Repeated Nodes has an Anchor (&) and a alias (*) that reference that Anchor node
# by using the name of the Anchor node. Anchor node name (&repeated) and alias (*repeated).
# We have two key: value-pairs. key has the value, Hi there, and new_key also has the value, Hi there.

key: &repeated Hi there
new_key: *repeated

# Our Anchor node, &person, has two key: value-pairs, name and age, plus a nested mapping, address.
# Anchor node, &person, node end at, streetname.
# The alias, *person, repeats and has the same value as the Anchor node.

person: &person
  name: Maaike
  age: 30
  address:
    streetname: Langstraat
    number: 1
    city: Amsterdam
    zipcode: 1234AB
    country: The Netherlands

another_person: *person


# another_person has the same values, Maaike and 30, as person.
# But what if we want to change another_person name.

person: &person
  name: Maaike
  age: 30

another_person: *person

# We can do a merge (<<) and repeat using the Anchor.
# yet_another_person now has a different name and the same age value.
person: &person
  name: Maaike
  age: 30

another_person: *person

yet_another_person: 
<<: *person
name: Freda

# For yet_another_person, we changed the name. But we can also add info for this person.

yet_another_person: 
<<: *person
name: Freda
address: { 
            streetname: YAML streetname, 
            number: 1 
         }
birthday: 1990-01-01
phone: !! str 061234567
description: <- We can use Multi-line Strings, folding and chopping to 
add a description about Freda's phone number. Her number is converted
into a string because Freda's number can be taken as a octal number.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
                     YAML Working with Tags
----------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2022-03-21 and 20 are converted into a string data type.
# The other 20 is already a integer, therefore, no need to convert.

no_date: !! str 2022-03-21
no_number: !! str 20
unnecessary: !! int 20

Tag	Data type	Example
| Tag        | Data type                  | Example                          |
|------------|----------------------------|----------------------------------|
| !!str      | String (text)              | value: !!str 123                 |
| !!int      | Integer (whole number)     | value: !!int 42                  |
| !!float    | Floating-point number      | value: !!float 3.14              |
| !!bool     | Boolean (true/false)       | value: !!bool true               |
| !!null     | Null (no value)            | value: !!null null               |
| !!seq      | Sequence (list/array)      | value: !!seq [red, blue]         |
| !!map      | Mapping (key-value pairs)  | value: !!map {name: Maaike}      |
| !!binary   | Binary data                | value: !!binary SGVsbG8=         |
